version: 2.0.0
components:
  skill-used-hook:
    description: 'v7.10.0 (Skills Phase 1) Claude Code PostToolUse hook (matcher: Skill) — scripts/claude-code-skill-used.sh, copied to the plugin as paradigm-skill-used.sh and installed by `paradigm hooks install --claude-code`. Pre-filters payloads without `pdg-`, then pipes the payload to the hidden `paradigm skill record-usage` verb, which appends a !skill-used row. Always exit 0, no output.'
    type: hook
    file: scripts/claude-code-skill-used.sh
    signals:
      - '!skill-used'
    tags:
      - hooks
      - skills
  delegation-hook:
    description: 'Claude Code PostToolUse hook (matcher: Agent|Task) — scripts/claude-code-delegation.sh, copied to the plugin as paradigm-delegation.sh and installed by `paradigm hooks install --claude-code`. Credits Agent-tool delegation to the orchestration gates (TD-2026-10-28-956): when tool_input.subagent_type is a team agent (`paradigm:*`, a project agent in <root>/.claude/agents, or a user-global agent in ~/.claude/agents such as atelier-mika; matched by <type>.md or `name:` frontmatter) AND tool_response is non-empty, writes <root>/.paradigm/.sessions/<session_id>.orchestrated (lasts for the session) and <root>/.paradigm/.orchestrated with the #orchestration-marker payload shape (source delegation, plus subagent; honoured by age). Markers go to the project root (#paradigm-resolve _pm_project_root), never a linked worktree. Always appends a delegation row (credited true|false) to .paradigm/events/team-funnel.jsonl. Subagent string sanitized to [a-zA-Z0-9:._-]. jq with grep fallback; fail open, always exit 0, no output. Hook-monitor label PostToolUse-Agent.'
    type: hook
    file: scripts/claude-code-delegation.sh
    components:
      - '#team-funnel'
      - '#orchestration-marker'
    signals:
      - '!delegation-recorded'
      - '!team-funnel'
    tags:
      - hooks
      - orchestration
      - team-funnel
  precommit-hooks:
    description: 'Retired pre-commit hooks (scripts/claude-code-precommit.sh PreToolUse Bash, scripts/cursor-precommit.sh beforeShellExecution). They used to rebuild the index on `git commit` and `git add` scan-index.json, navigator.yaml and flow-index.json, whose generatedAt stamp gave every commit an index diff (T-2026-10-28-002). Now no-ops that drain stdin and exit 0; the files stay because existing settings.json / hooks.json entries call them. The generated files are gitignored (#session-markers-gitignore); the MCP server serves a live index and SessionStart rebuilds on a fresh clone.'
    type: hook
    file: scripts/claude-code-precommit.sh
    tags:
      - hooks
  touched-files-ledger:
    description: 'Claude Code PostToolUse hook (matcher: Write|Edit) — scripts/claude-code-postwrite.sh, copied to the plugin as paradigm-postwrite.sh and installed by `paradigm hooks install --claude-code` (the installer upgrades the old never-matching ''Edit,Write'' matcher). Appends the edited file (tool_input.file_path or notebook_path), relative to the project root (logical or physical path), to <root>/.paradigm/.sessions/<session_id>.touched, deduped. The Claude Code Stop hook judges only `git diff --name-only HEAD` files in this ledger; other changed files (Bash edits, other sessions, worktrees) are listed as an advisory and never block. Without a session id the Stop hook judges the whole tree. Always exit 0, no output. Hook-monitor label PostToolUse-Write.'
    type: hook
    file: scripts/claude-code-postwrite.sh
    tags:
      - hooks
      - stop-hook
  paradigm-hooks:
    description: CLI hook installer and enforcement scripts for Claude Code, Cursor, and git. Stop hook validates 13 compliance checks including .purpose freshness, portal.yaml coverage, aspect anchors, and lore recording. Supports PARADIGM_AUTO_FIX=1 for auto-fixing trivial violations (missing lore entries); uncovered directories are filed as a purpose-coverage task (#purpose-coverage-queue) and still named in the block message, and no .purpose stub is ever written. Includes plugin version compatibility checking against semver ranges from hooks.json compatibleVersions field.
    type: command
    signals:
      - '!compliance-violation'
      - '!hook-installed'
  hook-monitor:
    description: |
      7.15.2 hook self-monitoring (scripts/paradigm-hook-monitor.sh, sourced by
      every Claude Code hook via _pm_begin <Hook> <timeoutMs> <budgetMs>).
      Appends a start record and, from an EXIT trap, an end record (wall ms,
      rc, outcome ok|blocked|error|killed; SIGTERM/HUP/INT -> killed) to the
      ring .paradigm/events/hook-timings.jsonl. A SIGKILLed hook leaves a
      start with no end; readers treat it as killed once older than its
      timeout + 5 s. Cost: two date(1) calls and two shell appends, no CLI or
      node spawn. Ring trimmed to PARADIGM_HOOK_RING_MAX (500) lines on ~1 in
      64 runs and deterministically at SessionStart. Fail open: no stdout,
      exit code preserved, every write best-effort; PARADIGM_HOOK_MONITOR_OFF=1
      disables it. Ring + marker are in the #session-markers-gitignore block.
      Per-section timing (8.0): _pm_mark <section> closes the open section and
      opens the next; _pm_count <name> <int> adds a counter. At exit ONE record
      per Stop run goes to .paradigm/events/stop-timing.jsonl (total ms,
      outcome, "inflight" = the section still open when the hook ended, so a
      killed run names where it was stuck, every section's ms, counters
      modified/source/purpose_files/anchors/pending_review/generated_only).
      Capped to PARADIGM_STOP_TIMING_MAX (200) lines on the ring's trim cadence.
      Cost: one date(1) per mark (~22 per Stop). paradigm-common.sh defines
      no-op fallbacks, so the Cursor hook (no monitor) is unaffected.
    type: utility
    file: scripts/paradigm-hook-monitor.sh
    tags:
      - hooks
      - observability
  hook-health-warning:
    description: |
      7.15.2 SessionStart hook (scripts/claude-code-session-start.sh, plugin
      paradigm-session-start.sh, hooks.json SessionStart, timeout 10 s). One
      awk pass over the #hook-monitor ring; for each hook killed (start with no
      end older than timeout + 5 s, or a recorded SIGTERM) or over its budget
      since the last warning it prints ONE line to stdout (added to the agent's
      context), e.g. "Paradigm Stop hook timed out 11 time(s) since <date>;
      enforcement did not run. Run `paradigm doctor`." Once per session
      (.paradigm/.hook-health-warned = "<session_id> <ms>"); also trims the
      ring to its cap. Always exits 0. Registered by the plugin hooks.json AND by
      the project-level `paradigm hooks install --claude-code` (.claude/settings.json).
      Fresh-clone rebuild (T-2026-10-28-002): when .paradigm/scan-index.json or
      navigator.yaml is missing (generated state is gitignored by
      #session-markers-gitignore) it starts `paradigm index --quiet` through
      #paradigm-resolve, fully detached; cursor-session-start.sh does the same.
    type: hook
    file: scripts/claude-code-session-start.sh
    tags:
      - hooks
      - observability
  hook-syntax-validation:
    description: |
      validateBashSyntax (index.ts) runs `bash -n` on every hook before `paradigm hooks install` writes them; only
      a real syntax report (syntax error / unexpected EOF or token) blocks. No usable bash (Windows without Git
      Bash on PATH, or the WSL stub bash.exe with no distro) counts as "can't validate", so install still works
      there. Shipped .sh hooks, plugin .cjs scripts and generated-hooks.ts are pinned to LF in .gitattributes
      (a CRLF checkout breaks bash and generate-hooks --check); the Windows workflow asserts it. The script is
      written into a fresh fs.mkdtempSync directory and checked with execFileSync('bash', ['-n', path]), no shell.
    type: utility
    file: index.ts
    tags:
      - hooks
      - windows
  git-hook-state:
    description: |
      git-hook-state.ts: classes .git/hooks/post-commit and pre-push as absent,
      foreign (no "# Paradigm <name> hook" + "# Installed by: paradigm hooks
      install" header: never touched), outdated (paradigm-hook-version below
      GIT_HOOK_VERSION = 3, no marker = v1, or still running `npx paradigm`) or
      current. `paradigm hooks install` upgrades an outdated Paradigm hook
      without --force, keeping the old file as <name>.paradigm-v<N>.bak so
      local edits survive; a current one is left alone; `hooks status` and
      `paradigm doctor` report outdated ones. Free of chalk/generated-hooks so
      doctor loads it cheaply. Bump GIT_HOOK_VERSION with the hook bodies'
      marker in index.ts.
    type: utility
    file: git-hook-state.ts
    tags:
      - hooks
      - git
  paradigm-resolve:
    description: |
      Sourced POSIX-sh helper (scripts/paradigm-resolve.sh, copied to both
      plugins and written by `paradigm hooks install` beside paradigm-common.sh;
      the git post-commit / pre-push hooks embed it, hook version 3) that is the
      one place the hooks find the paradigm CLI. Used by paradigm-common.sh
      (Stop hooks, project root = the caller's $CWD), both pre-commit hooks
      (project root = CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR / Cursor input cwd, then the git
      toplevel, then pwd; the hook cd's there before indexing) and the git hooks
      (git toplevel). `_pm_resolve_paradigm <root>` normalises the root with
      `CDPATH= cd -P -- root && pwd -P` and sets PARADIGM_BIN to a single
      command word: (1) the project's own @a-company/paradigm: node runs the
      `paradigm` bin entry read from
      <root>/node_modules/@a-company/paradigm/package.json (never whatever
      another package put at node_modules/.bin/paradigm), the root only (never
      a parent or the subdirectory the hook ran from), never a copy committed to
      git (`git ls-files` non-empty), and not when the root has a .git that git
      fails on (dubious ownership, no git): it cannot be checked; quiet outside
      a repo. No npx, because npm 11 applies the project's devEngines to npx
      and a pnpm-only repo fails EBADDEVENGINES; (2) `paradigm` on PATH. No npx
      step (Aegis P1). The plugin's MCP launcher (#paradigm-mcp-launch) uses the
      same order. Aegis L3 is narrowed, not dropped: only @a-company/paradigm
      at the project root runs (the trust of the workspace itself);
      paradigm-skill-used.sh stays PATH-only (no project root).
      Project root and session state (T-2026-10-27-005): `_pm_project_root
      <cwd>` sets PM_ROOT to $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR (when it holds .paradigm/),
      else the main checkout (parent of `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`), else
      <cwd>; a linked worktree the session did not start in is judged at the
      root. `_pm_session_id` reads session_id (sanitised) into PM_SID.
      Per-session files live in <root>/.paradigm/.sessions/<id>.<kind>
      (touched ledger, orchestrated, stop-fp, team-prompted, team-reminded);
      `_pm_gate_resolved` honours the session's orchestrated file or a fresh
      shared .orchestrated / .solo-declared (TTL); `_pm_once` caps a reminder
      to once per session (per TTL window without a session id).
    type: utility
    file: scripts/paradigm-resolve.sh
    components:
      - '#paradigm-mcp-launch'
    tags:
      - hooks
  stop-hook-timing:
    description: |
      7.15.2 timing contract for every plugin hook. Claude Code waits for EOF on
      a hook's stdout/stderr, not for its exit, until the hook timeout (Stop: 30 s).
      Rules: (1) never background a command without </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1;
      (2) paradigm-common.sh discovers .purpose files via `git ls-files -co
      --exclude-standard` (nested agent worktrees and node_modules are skipped)
      and keeps hot loops fork-free (_parent, _is_modified, one awk pass for
      Check 4 anchors); (3) no network: the CLI resolves through
      scripts/paradigm-resolve.sh (#paradigm-resolve), never a fetch; (4) generated-state
      scope (8.0): when every modified file is .paradigm/* or .warpline/*,
      at the root or nested in a package (packages/x/.paradigm/events; the
      hooks, post-commit and MCP tools write these on every turn) no code
      changed, so purpose discovery (Checks 4/6) and the compliance-check CLI
      are skipped (~0.35 s instead of ~1.2-1.9 s); remediations, purpose-required
      patterns and the memory nudge still run. Generated state (root or nested)
      never counts as a source file (it inflated the orchestration magnitude).
      Enforced by tests/hooks-timing.test.ts (each hook under 2 s through a
      pipe, plus a lint check on background jobs) and tests/hooks-monitor.test.ts.
    type: utility
    file: scripts/paradigm-common.sh
    tags:
      - hooks
      - performance
  stop-loop-guard:
    description: |
      8.0.2 Stop-hook loop guard (claude-code-stop.sh, cursor-stop.sh; helpers
      _pm_violation_fp and _pm_record_unresolved in paradigm-common.sh). A
      violation the agent cannot fix inside the turn used to block every stop
      forever. Claude Code: the hook reads stop_hook_active (jq, grep
      fallback); on a blocked Stop it fingerprints the violation set (headline
      "  - " lines, digits stripped, sorted, cksum) and keeps it in the marker
      .paradigm/.sessions/<session_id>.stop-fp (.paradigm/.stop-block-fingerprint
      without a session id; line 1 fingerprint, line 2 consecutive blocks).
      Loop cap (T-2026-10-27-005): a violation set blocks once. When
      stop_hook_active is true AND the fingerprint matches (the fix attempt
      changed nothing) OR the count is already 3, it prints the violations
      under "unresolved after a fix attempt — ending the turn", appends a
      stop-block-unresolved row (fingerprint, violations, consecutive) to
      .paradigm/events/team-funnel.jsonl and exits 0, leaving the marker. On a
      later turn the identical set is advisory ("already reported this
      session"), exit 0. A changed set writes the marker (count+1 when active,
      else 1) and exits 2. Cursor: RETRY_COUNT comes from
      the input's loop_count when present, else the .stop-hook-active file
      counter; the same fingerprint rule runs before the 3-retry cap. Every
      pass (including no modifications) removes the marker. PARADIGM_OVERRIDE
      is unchanged (overridden remediations never reach VIOLATIONS). The marker
      is in the #session-markers-gitignore block. Tests:
      tests/hooks-stop-loop-guard.test.ts.
    type: utility
    file: scripts/claude-code-stop.sh
    signals:
      - '!team-funnel'
    tags:
      - hooks
      - stop-hook
      - team-funnel
  stop-hook-purpose-coverage:
    description: >-
      Stop hook Checks 1 and 2 (paradigm-common.sh, section
      purpose-coverage-resolve) use the shared #purpose-coverage rule (T-2026-10-27-003):
      one `paradigm purpose coverage <dirs...>` call (~150 ms) scores every
      modified source directory; _covering_purpose reads the tab-separated
      answer. Covered = own .purpose or an ancestor that documents the dir;
      placeholder stubs never count. Check 1 flags a covered dir whose
      covering .purpose was not updated; Check 2 flags dirs no .purpose
      documents. When the CLI cannot answer (missing, or an older CLI) it falls
      back to the nearest-.purpose walk; the coverage_shared counter records
      which rule ran. Under PARADIGM_AUTO_FIX=1 the same call adds --queue
      (and Check 11 purpose-required calls --require-own --queue): uncovered dirs
      become one deduped purpose-coverage task, the block message names the dirs
      and the task id, and nothing is written into the tree. Check 5 skips the
      JSON lines Cid appends to .pending-review for new-symbol areas (they are not
      file paths). Coverage gaps are never written there; the purpose-coverage
      task is their only record.
      Tests: tests/hooks-stop.test.ts (shared rule and auto-fix cases).
    type: utility
    file: scripts/paradigm-common.sh
    components:
      - '#purpose-coverage'
    tags: [hooks, stop-hook, coverage]
  has-compliance-claimant:
    description: |
      v6.0.4 POSIX sh helper in paradigm-common.sh that reads
      .paradigm/roster.yaml and sets HAS_COMPLIANCE_CLAIMANT=true iff the
      'active:' list contains 'compliance' (block-list or inline-array form).
      Falls back to false on missing/unparseable. Wave 1 introduces the
      helper only — no callers yet (Wave 2 wires Check 6 and Check 10 to
      consume it).
    type: utility
    file: scripts/paradigm-common.sh
    tags:
      - hooks
      - roster
      - v6.0.4
  team-invocation-gates:
    description: |
      Pillar 0 (invocation reliability) advisory tier — two new Claude Code
      hooks: prompt-gate (UserPromptSubmit; classifies the incoming prompt and
      injects a decision-time orchestration directive into model context, once
      per TTL window, telemetry on every match) and team-gate (PreToolUse
      Write|Edit; one-time stderr advisory before source edits in unresolved
      sessions). Both read markers at the project root (#paradigm-resolve) and
      honour a team-agent delegation this session
      (.paradigm/.sessions/<id>.orchestrated, lasts for the session), fresh
      shared .orchestrated / .solo-declared markers (by AGE,
      PARADIGM_GATE_TTL_HOURS, default 4h), and the PARADIGM_TEAM_GATE=off kill
      switch. Each reminder fires once per session (.sessions/<id>.team-prompted
      / .team-reminded; per TTL window without a session id). The copy
      recommends delegating to a team agent with the Agent tool, or `paradigm
      solo`; paradigm_orchestrate_inline does not satisfy the gate (T-2026-10-27-005).
      Markers are never stop-cleared, because Stop fires per assistant turn
      (Check 13 reads the same markers). Check 13 in paradigm-common.sh records deduped
      bypass events to team-funnel telemetry at both warn and block severity.
    type: utility
    components:
      - '#team-funnel'
    signals:
      - '!team-funnel'
    tags:
      - hooks
      - orchestration
      - team-funnel
      - pillar-0
    file: scripts/claude-code-prompt-gate.sh
  check-14-remediation-gate:
    description: |
      v6.1 Sprint 1 Wave 2 — Stop hook Check 14. Iterates the cached
      _REMEDIATIONS_JSON (sourced from `paradigm internal active-remediations
      --json`). severity=guard adds to VIOLATIONS (blocks); advise/auto-author
      route to ADVISORY. Honors PARADIGM_OVERRIDE comma-list to skip ids and
      append an audit row to .paradigm/events/overrides.jsonl with mechanism=env.
      Iteration uses temp-file + redirect (NOT pipe) for POSIX subshell-safe
      VIOLATION_COUNT propagation.
    type: utility
    file: scripts/paradigm-common.sh
    signals:
      - '!override-event'
    tags:
      - hooks
      - remediation
      - soft-block
      - v6.1
  check-15-memory-hygiene-nudge:
    description: |
      Memory Steward A3 (TD-2026-09-19-110) — Stop hook Check 15. Advisory-only
      cheap heuristic that nudges `paradigm memory review` when native Claude
      Code memory (outside the repo at $HOME/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/)
      looks due. Metadata only (entry count > 25, MEMORY.md bytes > 32768, days
      since .paradigm/.memory-last-review > 30, or never-reviewed + entries > 15)
      — NO LLM, NO scan, NO content reads. Appends at most one line to ADVISORY;
      NEVER touches VIOLATIONS. Fail-open on missing HOME/dir/stat: emits nothing.
      <slug> is Claude Code's project key: every character of $CWD other than
      A-Z a-z 0-9 becomes '-' (Windows C:\Users\me\repo -> C--Users-me-repo;
      under Git Bash a /c/... $CWD is first converted with cygpath -w). Before
      PR #63 only '/' was replaced, so Windows paths (and POSIX paths with '.',
      '_' or spaces) never found the memory dir. Same rule as nativeMemorySlug
      in paradigm-mcp learning-stores.ts.
    type: utility
    file: scripts/paradigm-common.sh
    tags:
      - hooks
      - memory-steward
      - advisory
      - loid

aspects:
  ~stop-hook-8-compliance-checks:
    description: |
      Stop hook blocks agent completion unless 8 checks pass:
      (1) source files modified without .purpose updates (threshold 2+),
      (2) modified directories missing .purpose files entirely,
      (3) route patterns added without portal.yaml,
      (4) aspect anchor files that no longer exist,
      (5) per-directory .purpose freshness via .pending-review,
      (6) aspect coverage advisory,
      (7) lore entry expected for 3+ source file sessions,
      (8) blocking habits not satisfied
    category: rule
    severity: critical
    tags: [hooks, compliance, enforcement]
    anchors:
      - generated-hooks.ts:7-24
    applies-to:
      - "#paradigm-hooks"

  ~pre-tool-use-auto-reindex:
    description: PreToolUse hook intercepts Bash calls matching git commit patterns and automatically runs paradigm reindex before the commit proceeds; never blocks the tool call
    category: rule
    severity: low
    tags: [hooks, reindex]
    anchors:
      - generated-hooks.ts:7-7
    applies-to:
      - "#paradigm-hooks"

  ~post-tool-use-advisory:
    description: PostToolUse hook fires after Edit/Write tool calls and emits an advisory reminder when source files are edited without nearby .purpose coverage; advisory only, never blocks
    category: rule
    severity: low
    tags: [hooks, advisory]
    anchors:
      - generated-hooks.ts:7-7
    applies-to:
      - "#paradigm-hooks"
